.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd October 8, 2026
.Dt INTRO 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm intro
.Nd introduction to ABSD and its commands
.Sh DESCRIPTION
You are in a session on ABSD, a small x86-64 operating system.
The session is an SSH connection to
.Nm sshd ,
the SSH server, which started
.Xr absd-sh 1
for you (or, for
.Ql ssh host command ,
started that one command directly).
Everything you read or write goes through one file server; see
.Xr filesystem 7 .
.Pp
ABSD is not a Unix.
Programs are started, never forked; a process holds handles with rights
instead of file descriptors and user IDs; there are no signals, no users,
and no permission bits.
The session is a single operator identity whose authority is fixed by
.Nm sshd
when the session starts; everything the session runs has that authority or
less.
See
.Xr process 7
and
.Xr handles 7 .
.Pp
Start with
.Xr afterboot 8 .
.Ss Commands under /bin
A bare command name runs
.Pa /bin/ Ns Ar name .
There is no
.Ev PATH .
The platform's own programs:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width "sha256sum" -compact
.It Nm cat
copy files, or standard input, to standard output
.It Nm cp
copy files and directories
.Pq Fl r
.It Nm date
the date and time, UTC; see
.Xr date 1
.It Nm df
filesystem space, and this session's mounts
.It Nm echo
print arguments
.It Nm head
first lines or bytes of files
.It Nm ls
list directories;
.Fl l
adds kind, read-only flag, links, and size
.It Nm man
print a manual page; see
.Xr man 1
.It Nm mkdir
make directories
.Pq Fl p
.It Nm mv
rename, or move into a directory, in one filesystem commit
.It Nm printenv
print the environment (empty in an SSH session)
.It Nm pwd
print the working directory
.It Nm release
the release this machine is serving; see
.Xr release 1
.It Nm rm
remove files, and with
.Fl r
directories
.It Nm sha256sum
SHA-256 of files, or check a list
.Pq Fl c
.It Nm stat
what the filesystem records about an object
.It Nm sync
commit everything the file server has accepted
.It Nm sysinfo
the release, this boot, the kernel's memory and object counts, and the
filesystem's space, live; see
.Xr sysinfo 1
.It Nm tail
last lines or bytes of files
.It Nm uname
system name, and with
.Fl a
or
.Fl r
the release id
.It Nm uptime
time since boot, and this boot's identity; see
.Xr uptime 1
.It Nm wc
line, word, and byte counts
.El
.Pp
Unmodified uutils 0.12.0 programs, built from their published sources:
.Nm basename ,
.Nm base64 ,
.Nm cut ,
.Nm fold ,
.Nm nl ,
.Nm seq ,
and
.Nm uniq .
They behave as on Linux.
And
.Nm rg ,
ripgrep 15.2.0, likewise unmodified: the search tool (there is no
.Nm grep ) ;
see
.Xr rg 1
for what differs here, in particular that standard input must be named as
.Ql - .
Each port is 0.5 to 2.3 MB, and a program is read whole through the file
server before it starts, so they start more slowly than the native commands.
.Pp
.Nm man
has pages only for the topics listed under
.Sx SEE ALSO ;
most commands have no page of their own.
.Ss Deliberately absent
These are not missing by accident; each would need a mechanism or an
authority decision ABSD does not have:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Nm kill ,
.Nm top ,
a
.Pa /bin/ps :
a process can see only itself and its own children; the shell's
.Xr ps 1
builtin asks
.Nm sshd
for this session's processes only.
.It
.Nm chmod ,
.Nm chown ,
.Nm touch ,
.Nm ln :
there are no owners, modes, or links, and nothing sets a file's time
(the one time a file records is described in
.Xr filesystem 7 ) .
.It
.Nm syslogd ,
.Nm cron ,
.Pa /etc/rc.d :
each program that logs writes its own file under
.Pa /var/log ;
nothing runs on a schedule; the boot starts a fixed set of programs.
.It
.Nm mount :
a session's mounts are fixed by
.Nm sshd .
.It
Users, logins, and
.Nm su :
one operator identity; multi-session is not multi-user.
.It
A screen editor
.Pq Nm vi , Nm nano :
a program's keyboard is its standard input, which on a terminal session
the SSH server edits a line at a time (no raw mode, no window size);
see
.Xr absd-sh 1 .
.It
.Nm fork ,
.Nm exec ,
signals, job control, process groups: see
.Xr process 7 .
.El
.Pp
There is no text editor and no pager (above).
Write files with
.Nm echo
and
.Ql > ,
or with
.Nm cp ;
read long output a part at a time with
.Nm head
and
.Nm tail ,
or pick lines out of it with
.Nm rg .
.Ss Copying files
There is no
.Nm scp
or
.Nm sftp
(the SSH server has no subsystems).
Copy with a command and redirection instead, from the host:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ssh -p 2222 operator@127.0.0.1 'cat > /home/operator/f' < f
$ ssh -p 2222 operator@127.0.0.1 cat /home/operator/f > f
.Ed
.Pp
Both are byte-exact for any file (binary included), as long as no terminal
is requested (no
.Fl t ) .
See
.Xr afterboot 8 .
.Sh QUALIFICATION
What these pages describe is the qualified system: the ABSD kernel's
.Ql net fsd
durable boot under QEMU (x86-64, TCG,
.Ql -cpu max,vendor=GenuineIntel,+rdrand ,
one e1000 network card, one IDE disk image) running the file server
.Nm absd-fsd
and
.Nm sshd ,
reached from the host with OpenSSH.
The platform's
.Pa scripts/check-all.sh
qualifies it.
No ABSD boot has run on physical hardware.
The operator image is built and booted by the platform's
.Pa scripts/build-operator-image.sh
and
.Pa scripts/boot-operator.sh ;
see
.Xr afterboot 8 .
